common/ library). Unit testing is only clean because parse_point etc. are separated from main(). If the logic were still inline in main(), unit testing would be impossible. This is a nice \"aha\" about their own architecture.\n\nLet me now also consider: do I need to ask clarifying questions? Yes, a few:\n1. Scope of the first unit test: which functions? (propose parse_point C+C++, or also parse_int32, trim/is_empty/is_hex_prefix?)\n2. Do they want me to plan it as a full thing now, or first just understand + do a micro-practice themselves?\n\nBut actually the user's current message is specifically: \"для начала расскажи мне в чём смысл юнит теста, как он устроен\". So the PRIMARY deliverable is the explanation. They said \"хочу его сделать\" (want to make it), but \"для начала расскажи\" (first tell me). So I should lead with the explanation, confirm layer 6, and then offer to plan the actual implementation (with clarifying questions).\n\nLet me keep the explanation tight but substantive. I'll structure:\n\n1. Что это и зачем (смысл) — with contrast to their acceptance tests.\n2. Как устроен (структура) — with a tiny concrete sketch using their parse_point/parse_int32.\n3. Подтверждение: да, это слой 6 (6a), §4.8 голая версия.\n4. Архитектурные оси (per §4.2) before proposing.\n5. Questions to decide scope.\n\nLet me write this. I'll aim for clarity over brevity, but not bloated. Use their real code.\n\nLet me write concrete mini-example of the naked check. For C++:\n\n```cpp\n// tests/unit/unit_parse.cpp  (набросок)\n#include \"geometry.hpp\"\n#include <print>\n\nstatic int g_failures = 0;\n\nvoid check_point(std::string_view in, std::expected<Point, number_error> want) {\n    auto got = parse_point(in);\n    if (got != want) {\n        std::println(stderr, \"FAIL parse_point({}) ...\", in);\n        ++g_failures;\n    }\n}\n\nint main() {\n    check_point(\"1 2 3\", Point{1, 2, 3});\n    check_point(\"1 2\", std::unexpected(number_error::too_few));\n    // ...\n    return g_failures == 0 ? 0 : 1;\n}\n```\n\nBut note: `got != want` requires Point to have `operator==`, which it doesn't currently (struct with no operator==). In C++20+, `!=` for aggregate... no, defaulted == isn't generated unless declared. Actually `Point` is a simple aggregate; `std::expected<Point, ...> != std::expected<Point, ...>` requires `Point` to be comparable. They'd need `bool operator==(const Point&) const = default;` added to Point, OR compare fields manually. This is a real detail worth flagging — it's an architectural touch: adding `operator==` to Point is a reasonable, small change, and it's exactly the kind of thing a unit test forces you to notice. Good teaching point.\n\nFor C, the check would be:\n\n```c\n#include \"geometry.h\"\nstatic int failures = 0;\nvoid check_point(const char* in, int want_code, double x, double y, double z) {\n    struct Point p = {0};\n    int code = parse_point((char*)in, &p);  // note: non-const char* smell\n    if (code != want_code || (want_code==0 && (p.x!=x || ...))) { ... }\n}\n```\n\nNote the C version: parse_point takes `char*` (non-const), and mutates the input? Let me check — does it mutate str? Looking at geometry.c parse_point, it uses strtod(pointer, &end) and advances `pointer`, never writes to *str. So the string isn't mutated; the non-const char* is just because strtod's first arg is non-const historically. So in the test you pass a string literal cast to char* or a mutable buffer. Minor smell to note.\n\nOK. This is enough detail. Let me also flag the float comparison issue: for Point equality, coordinates are exact doubles from parsing (\"1 2 3\" → exactly 1.0, 2.0, 3.0), so `==` is fine for these. But in general float comparison needs tolerance (§6.1 note). For parse_point results, exact equality is fine because parsing \"1.5\" gives exactly 1.5 (representable). So `==` OK here. Worth a one-line note.\n\nNow, let me write the final answer. I should be careful: this is plan mode, so I should NOT write files. I'm explaining and then proposing a plan + questions.\n\nLet me also reconsider: is there any value in noting that gtest_smoke already exists and the \"naked\" unit test is the \"голая\" predecessor that §4.8 says should've existed from day 1? Yes, briefly.\n\nLet me draft the response now. I'll write in Russian. Keep it well-organized with headers.\n\nActually, let me reconsider length. The user explicitly asked for explanation (\"расскажи мне в чём смысл юнит теста, как он устроен\"). This justifies a fuller answer. But I should still be disciplined. I'll use compact bullets and one code sketch.\n\nLet me finalize.\n\nKey points to include in the plan/questions section:\n- Scope decision (which functions first)\n- Two exes (C + C++) mirroring\n- Static lib linkage + ASan wiring detail (verify)\n- operator== for Point (C++) needed\n- C non-const char* note\n- CTest registration (add_test + the unit test returns honest code)\n- The \\v/\\f regression they postponed → belongs here\n\nI'll ask a focused question about scope and about whether they want me to draft the full plan now.\n\nLet me write it.",
