          "text": "The user asks two things:\n1. Show how to write `operator==` for `Point` (C++ side)\n2. What does it mean \"тесты подсветят\" — the C signature `parse_point(char*, ...)` non-const pointer\n\nThese are explanation questions, read-only. I'm in plan mode. I should explain, show code (not write files). Let me answer concisely but with enough detail for a teaching context.\n\nFor #1: show the `operator==` for Point in geometry.hpp. There are options:\n- `bool operator==(const Point&) const = default;` — C++20 defaulted equality.\n- Or non-member `operator==`.\n\nGiven they're C++23, the defaulted member comparison is idiomatic. But note: Point is a simple struct with 3 doubles. Adding `operator==` as member with `= default` is the cleanest C++20 idiom.\n\nImportant caveat: floating point equality. For parse results it's exact. But should I mention that a defaulted `==` compares doubles exactly? Yes — I already noted parse gives exact values. But worth flagging: this `==` is exact bit-for-bit double comparison, which is correct for parse_point results (same literal → same value), but would be a footgun if someone later uses it for computed geometry (distance, centroid). That's an important teaching note.\n\nAlso mention: there's a subtlety — where to put it. In geometry.hpp inside struct. But wait — does adding `operator==` affect the DLL/static lib? No, it's inline in header, no ABI issue.\n\nActually, let me think about whether `operator==` should be `= default` or hand-written. `= default` requires C++20. They're on `/std:c++latest` so C++23 is fine. Defaulted member `operator==` generates field-wise comparison including implicit `!=` (C++20). Good.\n\nShow exact code:\n\n```cpp\nstruct Point {\n    double x;\n    double y;\n    double z;\n\n    bool operator==(const Point&) const = default;\n};\n```\n\nThis gives `==` and `!=` both.\n\nNote the ordering/naming: defaulted comparison compares all members in declaration order. Fine.\n\nCaveat about float exactness and NaN: two NaN values are never equal (NaN != NaN). But parse_point rejects non-finite (not_finite), so Point from parse_point never contains NaN/inf. So `==` is safe for parse results. Good — that's a nice consistency point: because parse_point guarantees finite, exact `==` is fine.\n\nFor #2: \"что значит тесты подсветят?\" — I need to explain what I meant by \"тесты это подсветят\". The C signature is:\n\n```c\nCOMMON_API int parse_point(char* str, struct Point* p);\n```\n\n`char*` is non-const. This means the function *declares* (by its type) that it might modify the string `str`. But the implementation doesn't actually modify it — it only reads. The non-const is forced by `strtod(char* nptr, char** endptr)` which takes non-const char* (historical C quirk: strtod's first param is non-const even though it doesn't modify the string).\n\nWhy does the unit test \"highlight\" this? Because when you write a unit test, you want to pass string literals or read-only data:\n\n```c\nparse_point(\"1 2 3\", &p);   // \"1 2 3\" is a const char[] literal\n```\n\nIn C, passing a string literal to a `char*` parameter is allowed (no warning, though it's technically UB if the function writes — but C compilers often don't warn). Actually in C it's legal to pass a string literal to char* (string literal is char[] in C, not const char[]). So in C this compiles without warning. But it's semantically wrong: the signature promises the callee may write, so a test author might think \"I need to give it a mutable buffer\" and write awkward code like `char buf[] = \"1 2 3\"; parse_point(buf, &p);` instead of clean `parse_point(\"1 2 3\", &p);`.\n\nSo \"тесты подсветят\" = the unit test, by wanting to call the function with literal/const inputs, exposes the smell that the signature over-promises mutation. It's a \"the type doesn't match the actual behavior\" smell that you only notice when you're the caller (which acceptance tests never are — they go through stdin).\n\nThe fix