eliverable here: explain both, show the code for #1, explain #2, and this feeds the plan. Since I'm in plan mode, I explain + show, but don't edit files.\n\nLet me be thorough but not bloated. Two sections.\n\nFor #1, I want to show exact code and mention:\n- C++20 `= default` idiom\n- It auto-generates both `==` and `!=`\n- Placement inside struct (member, no extra header needed)\n- Float caveat: exact comparison, NaN issue, but parse_point guarantees finite so safe for parse results; flag that if Point later holds computed values (centroid), exact `==` would be wrong, and you'd use tolerance instead — but that's a future concern.\n- [МНЕНИЕ] about whether member or hidden friend / non-member. Defaulted member is fine and idiomatic for a value-type aggregate. Mention hidden friend as the alternative that some prefer for symmetric types, but for Point the defaulted member is simplest.\n\nActually, let me think: is there a reason to make it a non-member (hidden friend) rather than member? For a simple aggregate of 3 doubles, defaulted member `operator==` is idiomatic and enough. Keep it simple. Don't over-engineer.\n\nAlso should note: this change touches geometry.hpp only (one line). No change to geometry.cpp. No ABI impact (inline in header).\n\nFor #2, explain precisely.\n\nLet me also, per anti-flattery, mark confidence. And maybe ask a small clarifying question at the end about whether they want to change the C signature (since it's a decision point). Actually they said \"давай начнём с правок\" — so they want to plan these edits. Let me give my recommendation clearly: \n\nFor #2, my recommendation: change to `const char*` and cast inside for strtod — because it's the honest type. But it touches geometry.h (declaration) and geometry.c (definition + internal cast). It's a small, safe change. But I should present it as a recommendation with the tradeoff, and note that strtod itself is non-const so you'd cast once at the call site with a comment.\n\nWait, is it worth it? Let me think about the value: const-correctness is genuinely valuable, and this is a teaching moment. The cast `(char*)pointer` inside parse_point (to satisfy strtod) is localized to one spot, documented. I'd recommend it. But it's a [МНЕНИЕ].\n\nLet me write the answer now.\n\nStructure:\n1. operator== — code + notes\n2. non-const char* — explanation + what \"подсветит\" means + fix recommendation\n\nKeep it readable. Use headers/bold. Not too long.\n\nLet me make sure the code for #1 is exactly right for their geometry.hpp. Current struct:\n\n```cpp\nstruct Point {\n    double x;\n    double y;\n    double z;\n};\n```\n\nAdding:\n\n```cpp\nstruct Point {\n    double x;\n    double y;\n    double z;\n\n    bool operator==(const Point&) const = default;\n};\n```\n\nThat's it. `= default` on `operator==` in C++20 compares memberwise (x, y, z in order) and also declares `!=`.\n\nI should mention `#include` — no new include needed.\n\nAlso mention: if they later want to use `std::expected<Point,...> != ...`, both `==` and `!=` are needed; `= default` gives both.\n\nLet me write.\n\nFor the float note, be precise: [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ] defaulted == compares doubles with ==. Two identical literals parse to identical bits, so fine. NaN never occurs in parse results (parse_point rejects). If someone constructs Point with NaN manually, == yields false for equal NaN — but that's not the use case.\n\nAlright, writing.",
