          "text": "Two questions:\n1. What does `default` mean? Is it the same `==`?\n2. Is `bool operator==(const Point&) const = default;` only added to geom.hpp?\n\nLet me answer concisely.\n\nQuestion 1: `= default` means \"generate the implementation for me\". It's a request to the compiler to synthesize the function body using the default semantics. For `operator==`, the default semantics is memberwise comparison. So yes, functionally it IS the same `==` as writing `return x == o.x && y == o.y && z == o.z;` by hand — but the compiler writes it. It's not \"a special `==`\", it's the ordinary `==` with the body auto-generated.\n\nKey distinction to teach: `= default` vs `= delete` vs omitting. `= default` = compiler generates it. It's a \"defaulted\" definition.\n\nAlso worth noting: defaulted `operator==` in C++20 does memberwise comparison of ALL members in declaration order, and automatically provides `!=`.\n\nLet me explain the analogy: like \"противень с рецептом по умолчанию\" — you tell compiler \"make me this function the standard way\".\n\nQuestion 2: Yes, only in geometry.hpp. Because:\n- It's a member function defined inline in the struct, so the definition lives in the header.\n- geometry.cpp includes geometry.hpp, so it sees the definition — no need to add anything to .cpp.\n- string_utils.hpp/cpp don't need it (they don't compare Points).\n- No other file defines Point.\n\nBut wait — should I double-check: is Point also in geometry.h (C side)? Yes, geometry.h has struct Point too. But that's C, no operator==. So only geometry.hpp.\n\nAlso, does the DLL/static lib export matter? The operator== is inline in the header, it's not exported via COMMON_API, it's implicitly inline. That's fine — inline functions don't need dllexport. No ABI concern.\n\nOne nuance: since it's inside the struct, it's implicitly inline. No ODR issues across translation units (main.cpp and geometry.cpp both see it, but inline so fine).\n\nLet me also address: does the `.cpp` need anything? No.\n\nLet me keep it concise per the style guidance but this is a teaching context. Two clear answers.\n\nI'm in plan mode — read-only, just answering questions. Good.\n\nLet me write in Russian.",
