match. No cast needed.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtlety. After `strtod`, `end` points into the string `str`. `end` is `char*` but it aliases into the `const char*` buffer. That's a \"const-ness leak\" but it's how strtod's API works by design — `endptr` is `char**` for historical reasons. This doesn't cause a compile error; it's just how it is.\n\nThen later in the code: `pointer = end;` — assigning `char*` to `const char*` is fine (adding const).\n\nSo actually, changing to `const char*` requires NO cast anywhere. My earlier message's cast `(char*)pointer` was unnecessary and based on a wrong fact.\n\nI must correct this honestly. This is important — the anti-flattery block explicitly says to not trust assertions by default and to correct errors.\n\nLet me also double-check: is there any MSVC-specific weirdness where strtod's signature differs? No, MSVC follows the standard C signature: `double strtod(const char *strSource, char **endptr);`.\n\nSo my correction:\n\n1. I was wrong: strtod's first param is `const char *`, not `char *`.\n2. Therefore NO cast is needed. `const char* pointer` passes directly to strtod.\n3. The reason I (wrongly) suggested the cast was a false memory/assumption about strtod being non-const.\n\nActually, wait — let me reconsider. Is there ANY historical function that has non-const first arg? There are some like... actually no, the string-to-number functions all take const char* in C89+. The `char** endptr` is the non-const part (it's a pointer OUT param, but it's a pointer to char*, and the char* it points to... hmm).\n\nActually the subtle \"const-correctness wart\" of strtod is: `endptr` is `char **` (not `const char **`), which means you can write through `*endptr` and modify the buffer. But that's about `endptr`, not the first argument. So the historical wart is on the OUT param, not the IN param.\n\nBut this wart doesn't require a cast on our side either, because we declare `char* end` (non-const), which matches `char**`.\n\nSo the summary: to make `parse_point` take `const char*`, you just:\n1. Change declaration in geometry.h to `const char* str`.\n2. Change definition in geometry.c: `const char* pointer = str;` and keep `char* end = NULL;`.\n3. `strtod(pointer, &end)` — no cast.\n\nEverything else compiles because `pointer = end` (char* → const char*) is a widening conversion.\n\nNow, is there any place in geometry.c where the string is actually written? Let me check again. From my earlier read of geometry.c: no writes. `pointer = end;`, `++pointer;`, `strtod`, `isspace`. No writes. Good.\n\nSo I should give an honest correction and the corrected plan.\n\nAlso, the user's specific question: \"это чтобы из const char сделать каст к просто char?\" — The answer is: no cast is needed at all, because strtod already takes const char*. My earlier suggestion of a cast was wrong.\n\nLet me be honest and clear about this. This is a teaching moment where I demonstrate exactly what the anti-flattery block demands: admitting my error.\n\nLet me structure:\n1. Direct correction: I was wrong, strtod takes const char*.\n2. So no cast needed; here's what actually changes.\n3. The real \"wart\" is elsewhere (endptr being char**), but it doesn't affect us.\n\nLet me also verify the exact current code lines from geometry.c to be precise:\n\n```c\nint parse_point(char* str, struct Point* p) {\n    char* pointer = str;\n    char* end = NULL;\n    ...\n    d[i] = strtod(pointer, &end);\n```\n\nAnd there are two strtod calls (one in the main loop, one for the \"extra\" check). Both would just work with const char* pointer.\n\nLet me write the correction concisely.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether I should double check the C standard signature once more. C17 7.22.1.3 \"The strtod, strtof, and strtold functions\":\n\n```c\ndouble strtod(const char * restrict nptr, char ** restrict endptr);\n```\n\nYes, confirmed. `const char * restrict nptr`.\n\nSo my previous statement was factually wrong. I'll own it.\n\nConf