 - commit (per §4.11)\n\n7. **Important architectural notes / decisions to confirm**:\n   - Where the test exe goes: `tests/unit_parse_point.cpp` and `tests/unit_parse_point.c` (flat in tests/), or a subdirectory `tests/unit/`? Their existing tests are flat. I'll propose flat names `unit_parse_point.cpp`/`.c`. Ask if they prefer a subdir.\n   - Whether to build with ASan (ON). Confirm: yes.\n   - Static lib linkage (common_cpp_static / common_c_static) vs shared. Static preferred.\n   - Do they need the unit test in BOTH debug and release? Their presets produce debug and release. ctest runs in both. The unit test will run in both. Note: in Release, ASan is off (only /Zi), NDEBUG defined. The unit test itself doesn't use assert, so fine.\n\n8. **Naming**: unit test target names: `unit_parse_point_cpp` and `unit_parse_point_c`. Test names in CTest: maybe `unit_parse_point_cpp` / `unit_parse_point_c` (single test per exe, since the exe itself reports pass/fail via exit code).\n\nLet me also think about a subtle point: the naked test harness and \"honest exit code\". The exe returns 0 if all pass, nonzero if any fail. add_test just runs the exe; ctest checks exit code == 0. Good. No need for run_case/check here — the unit test IS self-contained (no stdin, no files, no comparison against expect files).\n\nBut wait — one consideration: should the unit test print anything on success? A single line \"all N cases passed\" to stderr or stdout is conventional and helps when running manually. On failure, print each failed case with input and expected vs got. I'll describe that.\n\nAlso — the comparison output for C++: when `got != want` fails, printing \"FAIL parse_point(\"1 2\")\" but the `expected` doesn't stream nicely. So the check helper just prints the input string and increments counter. Optionally print expected/got — but std::expected<Point, number_error> isn't directly printable without extra work. Keep it simple: print the input that failed + maybe the error category. I'll note this is a \"keep it minimal\" decision.\n\nFor the C++ side, one more consideration: `check_point` takes `std::string_view` and the expected. For the error-only cases, `std::unexpected(number_error::too_few)`. That's verbose. A cleaner approach: two helpers — `check_ok(s, Point)` and `check_err(s, number_error)`. That's more readable. I'll propose that.\n\nFor C: `check_ok(const char* s, double x, double y, double z)` and `check_err(const char* s, int want_code)`.\n\nAlso floating point exactness: since parse results are exact for literals, comparing `==` on doubles for the \"ok\" cases is fine. But for subnormal \"1e-320\", is it exact? 1e-320 as a double literal parses to the same subnormal as the program would parse \"1e-320\" → same value. Both the test literal `1e-320` (written in test code as double) and the parsed value go through the same strtod/from_chars. On MSVC, from_chars for \"1e-320\" gives a subnormal; the test's expected `1e-320` literal is also compiled as the same subnormal. So == should hold. But there's a subtlety: the test writes expected coordinates as C++ double literals like `1e-320`, while parse_point parses the string \"1e-320\". These should be bit-identical. I'll flag this as [ПРЕДПОЛОЖЕНИЕ, ВЫСОКИЙ] — actually it's a known-to-be-safe case if both use the same conversion, but worth a note: for subnormals, prefer comparing a couple known-safe cases or note the assumption. Actually to keep the first unit test robust, I'd suggest NOT asserting subnormal exact equality in the first pass, OR just include it as an error-class test (out_of_range for 1e-400) rather than exact-value. The subnormal \"success\" case \"1e-320\" is already covered in acceptance (t3_c_subnormal). For the unit test, I can include it but flag the exactness nuance.\n\nLet me keep the plan focused and note the float-exactness caveat for the subnormal case.\n\nNow let me also decide: should the C++ test and C test be in the same file? No — they're different languages (C++ vs C), separate files and separate targets, mirroring the whole project's dual-language discipline.\n\nLet me also consider: the C++ test needs `#include \"geometry.hpp\"` and `#include \"parse_codes.hpp\"` (for number_error). Actually geometry.hpp already includes parse_codes.hpp. So just geometry.hpp. And `<print>` for std::println, or `<cstdio>`. They use std::print/std::println per their style. But note: std::println to stderr requires `<print>`. Fine.\n\nThe C test needs `#include \"geometry.h\"` (which includes parse_codes.h) and `<stdio.h>`.\n\nInclude path: the tests are in `tests/`, common headers in `common/`. How do the tests include? Looking at how t3_bbox_c/main.c includes: `#include \"../common/geometry.h\"` — relative path. But the test would be in `tests/`, so `#include \"../common/geometry.h\"`. But does CMake set include directories? The libraries use target_include_directories? Let me check — common/CMakeLists.txt doesn't seem to set target_include_directories. The task mains use relative `../common/`. So the unit test should also use `#include \"../common/geometry.hpp\"`. I'll note this.\n\nActually, wait — should I check whether common has target_include_directories set so that linking would propagate include paths? From what I read, common/CMakeLists.txt does NOT call target_include_directories. So includes are relative. The test in tests/ would use `../common/geometry.hpp`. That's consistent with existing style.\n\nLet me verify by checking if there's a target_include_directories anywhere. From the files I've read, I don't see one. So relative includes it is. Fine — consistent with the project.\n\nNow, let me also think about whether to register the unit test exe with `configure_target(ON)` — but `configure_target` also does `target_compile_options ... /W4 /permissive-` etc. And it's designed for both product and test harnesses. For the unit test, `ON` means it gets Zi + ASan (Debug). Good.\n\nBut there's a nuance: `configure_target` has `DBG` param. For `run_case`, `check`, `gtest_smoke`, they call `configure_target(x OFF)`. For unit test we want `ON` because it links the ASan library. I'll state this explicitly and why.\n\nAlso note: on MSVC, linking an ASan static lib into an exe without ASan → LNK errors (unresolved __asan). So `ON` is required. On Linux (Clang/GCC), same — need -fsanitize=address at link. So `ON` required on both. Good — `configure_target(ON)` handles both branches.\n\nLet me also think about where to add the add_executable/add_test in tests/CMakeLists.txt. It should go after the gtest_smoke block (or near it). Order doesn't strictly matter for build, but logically group unit tests. I'll propose adding a new section \"=== UNIT TESTS ===\" after gtest_smoke.\n\nActually, one question: should the unit test be a single add_test per exe, or split into many? Since it's a naked harness (single exe runs all cases, returns 0/1), it's ONE add_test per language. That's the \"голая\" version. GoogleTest later splits. Good.\n\nNow let me also think: do we need `enable_testing()` already? Yes, root has `enable_testing()`. And `add_subdirectory(tests)` is after `add_test(format-check)`. Fine.\n\nLet me also consider the CHANGELOG / README angle — §4.12 versioning. The project has CHANGELOG.md (Keep a Changelog) per the ladder. But that's layer 12; the journal mentions \"не забыть версионирование после правки\". This might be a \"mention in plan\" item: update README's testing section to document the unit test and how to run it. And CHANGELOG entry. I'll include as optional/later steps, ask if they want it.\n\nActually, let me check if README documents tests. I haven't read README. I should probably read it to know what to update. But I'm in plan mode and can read. Let me read README.md to understand what's there. Actually, let me be efficient — I'll mention README/CHANGELOG updates as a step and ask whether they want them now or later. But it'd be better to check if README has a testing section.\n\nLet me read README.md quickly.\n\nActually, I think I have enough to present a solid plan. Let me read README.md to ground the \"documentation\" step, and also check the presets file (CMakePresets.json) to know the build commands for the verification step.\n\nLet me read README.md and CMakePresets.json.",
