std::println(stderr, ...)` requires `<print>` (C++23). On MSVC 19.37+ and GCC 14+, fine (already their baseline). I'll use std::println.\n\nFor C, `fprintf(stderr, ...)`.\n\nNow about the \"check\" output detail: I want to keep it minimal but useful. On failure, print input + (for C++) the error category via a small to-string, or just \"FAIL: <input>\". I'll suggest printing the input string and, for the error case, the expected vs actual code. Keep minimal.\n\nLet me also consider: does the C++ `std::expected<Point, number_error>` comparison `got != want` work? `got` is `std::expected<Point, number_error>`, `want` is `std::expected<Point, number_error>`. `operator!=` requires `Point` `==` (done) and `number_error` `==` (built-in for enum class). Yes. Good.\n\nNow, one more consideration about the `check_ok` helper signature: it takes `std::string_view` and `Point`. But the expected Point needs to be constructed. `Point{1, 2, 3}`. For the C++ test, calling `parse_point(\"1 2 3\")` returns `std::expected<Point, number_error>`, compare to `Point{1,2,3}`. But `check_ok` needs to build `std::expected<Point, number_error>{Point{...}}` to compare. Or simpler: `check_ok` internally does `auto got = parse_point(in); if (!got || *got != want) { ... }`. That avoids constructing expected explicitly. Good — I'll propose that.\n\nSo C++ helpers:\n```cpp\nstatic void check_ok(std::string_view in, Point want) {\n    auto got = parse_point(in);\n    if (!got || *got != want) { ++failures; std::println(stderr, \"FAIL ok: \\\"{}\\\"\", in); }\n}\nstatic void check_err(std::string_view in, number_error want) {\n    auto got = parse_point(in);\n    if (!got || got.error() != want) { ++failures; std::println(stderr, \"FAIL err: \\\"{}\\\"\", in); }\n}\n```\nWait — `check_err` should fail if `got` has a value (i.e., unexpectedly succeeded) OR the error differs. `if (!got || got.error() != want)` — if `got` has a value, `!got` is false, so we need to also detect \"unexpectedly succeeded\"