1 2 3 nan\" → too_much (per their model, nan after x y z = too_much, README line 12)\n- regression \\v/\\f: \"\\v0x10 2 3\" → not_number; \"\\f0X10 2 3\" → not_number\n\nI should note: the `\\v`/`\\f` regression is the explicit postponed item. And note that in C++ side the same bytes go through from_chars + isspace, and isspace captures \\v/\\f — so the C++ side should also reject. Actually, wait — the `\\v0x10` regression was about the C side `is_hex_prefix` using `strspn(ptr, \" \\t\\n\\r\")` missing \\v/\\f. On C++ side, is_hex_prefix equivalent is inline (from_chars rejects 0x naturally), and leading-space skipping uses isspace. So both sides should reject. The unit test should assert BOTH sides reject `\\v0x10`/`\\f0X10`. Good.\n\nNow — a subtle question: the C++ `parse_point` — does it skip `\\v`/`\\f` as whitespace? It uses `std::isspace` which on MSVC with \"C\" locale: isspace includes \\v (0x0B) and \\f (0x0C). Yes. So \"\\v0x10 2 3\": skip \\v, then \"0x10\" → from_chars parses \"0\" then stops at \"x\" → ec ok (parsed 0), ptr points at \"x\" which is not space → not_number. Good.\n\nFor C side: \"\\v0x10\" → is_hex_prefix(pointer) — now is_hex_prefix uses isspace (per journal 09-21/22 fix). Let me verify is_hex_prefix implementation. I haven't read string_utils.c. But the journal says it now uses `while (isspace(...))`. So \\v is skipped, then \"0x10\" detected as hex → NUMBER_NOT_NUMBER. Good. But I should note I haven't re-read string_utils.c to confirm the isspace fix is actually present in HEAD (the journal 09-22 describes a scary incident where the isspace fix got lost). Let me flag this: verify is_hex_prefix actually uses isspace, else the \\v regression test will fail (which is exactly the point of the regression test — but I want to know the current state). Actually — this is a good thing to check in the plan's verification step: run the regression case and see.\n\nActually, let me read string_utils.c to confirm the current state of is_hex_prefix