prefix(pointer)) return NUMBER_NOT_NUMBER;\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nBut this changes the loop structure and the `i != 3` check afterward. Also, the `is_hex_prefix` already skips whitespace internally (it does `while (isspace) ++ptr`). Hmm, this is getting into a refactor.\n\nActually, wait — there's a subtlety. Let me not over-engineer. The user asked me to add test cases. I found a real asymmetry. My job now is to:\n1. Report the finding clearly.\n2. Propose options and let the user decide (since fixing geometry.c is a production change, and per their workflow they make these decisions with reasoning).\n\nLet me present:\n- C++ passed (whitespace-only → too_few).\n- C failed on \"   \" → returns not_number (not too_few).\n- Root cause: strtod on whitespace-only input returns end==pointer → C treats it as not_number; C++ skips whitespace first and returns too_few.\n- Note: in practice this is masked because t3/t4 filter whitespace-only lines via is_empty before calling parse_point, so the CLI never exposes it. But the unit test (calling parse_point directly) exposes it.\n- Options: (1) fix geometry.c to return too_few for whitespace-only (match C++), (2) drop the \"   \" case, (3) keep asymmetric expectations.\n\nRecommendation: fix geometry.c (it's a genuine inconsistency; the fix is small and local).\n\nLet me ask the user which they prefer, with a recommendation. Actually, given the flow, I think I should present this concisely and ask.\n\nBut wait — should I just fix it myself? The user's instruction was \"добавь в юнит тесты все кейсы\". Adding cases revealed a bug. Fixing the bug is beyond \"add cases\", but it's the natural next step. Given the learning context, I should surface it and ask, not silently fix.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the nature of this. Is \"   \" → not_number actually WRONG, or is it a defensible interpretation? \n\nArgument that it's wrong: a string with only whitespace has no \"number\" but also no \"invalid character\". \"too few coordinates\" (недостаточно координат) is more accurate than \"нечисловые данные\" (non-numeric data). The C++ side and the empty-string case both treat \"no data\" as too_few.\n\nArgument that it's defensible: strtod couldn't parse a number from \"   \", and \"   \" isn't empty per se... but it's all whitespace.\n\nI think \"too_few\" is the correct classification. The asymmetry is a genuine minor bug.\n\nAlso, importantly: the empty string \"\" → too_few (both), but \" \" (one space) → not_number (C only). This inconsistency within the C code itself (empty vs whitespace-only giving different codes) is a smell.\n\nLet me present this clearly and recommend the fix, and ask for the go-ahead. I'll also note this is exactly the kind of thing the unit test is meant to catch (and it did).\n\nLet me also verify: is the C++ side also possibly inconsistent? C++ \"\" → too_few, \" \" → too_few. Both too_few. Consistent. So only C is inconsistent.\n\nLet me write the report. I'll be concise.\n\nActually, let me also double-check: are there any OTHER failures I should anticipate? The C test failed ONLY on \"   \" (1 failure). The C++ passed all. So the only issue is the whitespace-only case.\n\nLet me also confirm the full list compiled/ran correctly otherwise — the C test showed exactly 1 failure (\"   \"), C++ 0 failures. So all other cases pass on both sides. Good — this also empirically confirms my other expectations were correct (hex, overflow, underflow, subnormal 1e-320, infinity, nan, etc.).\n\nNow let me report and ask how to handle the \"   \" case.\n\nI'll present three options with a recommendation. Let me keep it tight.\n\nActually, per the anti-flattery block and the user's learning style, I should present the finding with the root cause clearly and ask for their decision. Let me do that.\n\nOne more consideration: should I also fix geometry.c right away to demonstrate? No — better to ask, because:\n1. It's a production code change.\n2. The user might prefer to drop the 