` semantics exactly).\\n\\nFor a test runner that needs precise redirection to files, `CreateProcess` + a correct quoting helper is the standard route. Or use a framework (boost::process) which solves exactly this.\\n\\n[����] Boost.Process / Python subprocess / GoogleTest's own process utilities all build a command line string with proper quoting on Windows. So it's normal and unavoidable.\\n\\nCould also mention: `CommandLineToArgvW` + its inverse. Actually, there's a known trick: `CommandLineToArgvW(L\\\"\\\", ...)` no. There's a well-known quoting algorithm. There's also the `ArgvQuote` function from MSDN sample \\\"Parsing C++ Command-Line Arguments\\\" / the one in `boost/process/detail/windows/cmd.hpp`. So: implement/lift the documented algorithm.\\n\\nNow, is \\\"᪫����\\\" itself the problem, or just the ad-hoc quoting? The ᪫���� is mandatory; the bug is that unquoted concatenation is lossy. So fix = correct quoting, not elimination.\\n\\nLet me also mention: on Linux, `execv` - that's already what you do; true vector, zero responsibility. So the asymmetry stays. You can't unify without a wrapper; but you can hide it behind one helper with two impls (`#ifdef _WIN32`), which you basically have.\\n\\n### Question 2: Radius argument error semantics\\n\\nTheir proposed approach:\\n- trim leading/trailing whitespace\\n- if single number \u001a accept\\n- if \\\"10k\\\" or \\\"10 4\\\" \u001a generic: \\\"�������� ࠤ���, �뫮 ����祭�:%s. � ����祭��� ��ப� �����㦥�� ��譨� ᨬ����\\\"\\n- if empty \u001a \\\"�������� ࠤ��� �� �� �� �� ����祭\\\"\\n\\nCritique. Let me think about what's good/bad.\\n\\nGood:\\n- Distinguishing empty (no arg) vs malformed. Empty is a distinct case (arg not given / empty string). Note: the task says \\\"��㬥�� �� ����� / �� �᫮ / �� ������⥫�� \u001a ��� 64\\\". They already have separate messages: `t4_radius_empty` = \\\"�������� ࠤ���; ��� ���祭�� �� �뫮 �������\\\" and `t4_radius_too_much` = \\\"��譨� ��㬥���\\\".\\n- Trimming edges is consistent with t