 So need explicit finite. They already have `RAD_INF` test \u001a `t4_radius_inf`. So their simplified description omits it, but implementation likely still has it. I should remind: \\\"���� �᫮\\\" must mean \\\"���� ����筮� �᫮, �।�⠢���� � double, > 0\\\", ���� `inf`/`1e400` ��᪮��.\\n\\n6. **Empty vs whitespace-only**: `\\\"   \\\"` (spaces only) after trimming \u001a empty. Is that \\\"�� �� ����祭\\\" or \\\"�� �᫮\\\"? Their proposal: empty after trim \u001a \\\"�� �� ����祭\\\". But there's a subtlety: `EMPTY_ARG=\\\"\\\"` (arg literally empty) is \\\"no value\\\". `\\\"   \\\"` (arg of spaces) - also effectively no value. Treating both as \\\"�� ����祭\\\" is reasonable. But note current test uses `t4_radius_empty` only for `\\\"\\\"`. Might add a test for `\\\"   \\\"`.\\n\\nWait - there's also \\\"argument not passed at all\\\" (argc==1). Currently `t4_c_radius_empty` uses `EMPTY_ARG=\\\"\\\"` which means argv[1]=\\\"\\\" i.e., an empty-string argument, not \\\"no argument\\\". Are these distinguished? The task says \\\"��㬥�� �� ����� / �� �᫮ / �� ������⥫�� \u001a ��� 64\\\". So \\\"�� �����\\\" (argc==1) and empty string are both usage. Do they produce the same message? Let me check: in t4 argv handling, if argc<2 \u001a probably message \\\"������ �� �� ��।��\\\" or uses same. Let me check main.c/main.cpp for t4 to see. That's relevant.\\n\\nLet me inspect t4 sources and expected files. Read-only.\\n\\n7. **Wording/\u0015 4.6 style**: \\\"�������� ࠤ���, �뫮 ����祭�:%s.\\\" - need space after colon, and per \u0015 4.6 format \\\"�� �� ����稫���, ����� ���祭�� ����祭�, ����� �����⨬�\\\". Their message gives expected (radius) and received. Good. Minor: `�뫮 ����祭�:%s` missing space. Also \\\"� ����祭��� ��ப� �����㦥�� ��譨� ᨬ����\\\" - could include which char, but they explicitly chose not to; acceptable.\\n\\n8. **Should inner-space `\\\"10 4\\\"` be treated as \\\"too many values\\\" instead?** Arguably for radius one could split by whitespace and if >1 token \u001a \\\"too many values\\\". That would unify with argc-level \\\"��譨� ��㬥���\\\"? But the shell already decided boundaries; a single arg with inner space is content. I'd keep it content error. But note the *semantic* difference from `2 3` (two args) remains, which they found \\\"����⭮\\\". This is the same issue. The clean resolution: it's fine, argv shape is input.\\n\\n9. **Error code**: all these are USAGE 64. Confirm.\\n\\nLet me read the current t4 code and expected files to give grounded advice. Let me read:\\n- t4_filter_c/main.c\\n- t4_filter_cpp/main.cpp\\n- common/geometry.c / .hpp parse functions\\n- tests/expect/t4_radius_* (decode cp1251)\\n\\nLet me find t4 dirs. t4_filter_c, t4_filter_cpp presumably. Let me glob.\\n\\nLet me do a batch of reads.\\n\\nActually, since I'm forming a plan, understanding current code is important. Let me explore.\\n\\nLet me list directories and read t4 main sources + expected radius messages.\\n\\nI'll use bash to decode cp1251 expected files, and read source files.\\n\\nLet me be efficient: run one bash to dump all t4 radius expected files decoded as cp1251, and read t4 sources via read tool.\\n\\nFirst find paths.\",\r\n\"command\": \"Get-ChildItem -Path \\\"t4_filter_c\\\",\\\"t4_filter_cpp\\\" -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Select-Object Directory,Name; Write-Output \\\"=== radius expects (cp1251) ===\\\"; foreach ($f in @(\\\"t4_radius_empty\\\",\\\"t4_radius_symbol\\\",\\\"t4_radius_too_much\\\",\\\"t4_radius_inf\\\",\\\"t4_radius_negative\\\",\\\"t4_radius_plus_abc\\\",\\\"t4_radius_overflow\\\",\\\"t4_radius_underflow\\\",\\\"t4_radius_hex\\\")) { $b=[IO.File]::ReadAllBytes(\\\"tests\\\\expect\\\\$f\\\"); $s=[Text.Encoding]::GetEncoding(1251).GetString($b); Write-Output (\\\"{0} => {1}\\\" -f $f, $s) }\"\r\n\"output\": \"\\r\\nDirectory                                         Name    \\r\\n---------                                         ----    \\r\\nC:\\\\Users\\\\User\\\\Desktop\\\\inobitec_stud\\\\t4_filter_c   mai