ritten by hand, so they're \"independent\" of parse_point in the sense that they're manually specified.\n\nThe risk: if the person writing both parse_point and the test has the same wrong mental model, both could agree on the wrong behavior. E.g., if parse_point had a bug where \"1 2 3 4\" returned too_few instead of too_much, and the test ALSO expected too_few (because the author thought \"4 numbers = too few\"? unlikely, but the point stands).\n\nSo the strongest negative control is: mutate the TESTED code in a way that's INDEPENDENT of the test author's expectations, and confirm the test catches it. Reverting the whitespace fix is a great example because:\n- The \"correct\" behavior (too_few for whitespace) was established independently (from the C++ side and from the spec).\n- Reverting the C fix makes C diverge, and the test (which expects too_few) should catch it.\n\nSo A is the best. Let me recommend A primarily.\n\nLet me write the answer concisely. The user asked two things; I'll answer both, and give a concrete recommendation with expected result.\n\nI should note I'm in plan mode and can't execute — I'll present the plan and let the user run it (or I run when allowed).\n\nLet me write.\n\nActually, let me reconsider one more time whether there's an even more \"correct\" mutation to suggest. The user might specifically want to verify:\n1. The harness's exit code is honest (non-zero on failure).\n2. The test actually asserts.\n\nBoth A and B cover both (a failing case → exit code 1 → ctest Failed). A additionally proves the test catches a real behavior change.\n\nI'll present:\n- Confirm both rules exist (cite).\n- Recommend the negative control: \n  - Primary: revert the `\"   \"` fix in geometry.c → expect `parse_point_c` red with `FAIL err: \"   \"`.\n  - Secondary (if you want to also verify harness honesty independently): flip one expected value (`Point{1,2,3}` → `Point{1,2,4}`).\n- Expected exact output.\n\nKeep it tight. Let me write.",
