ый вопрос: неверен либо тест, либо поведение\". So when something fails, you attach the red output AND investigate/diagnose — don't just report \"всё упало\".\n\nAlso there's the distinction between:\n- A red run that's INTENTIONAL (negative control — you spoiled something on purpose to prove the test catches it): attach both green AND red.\n- A red run that's UNEXPECTED (something actually broke): attach the red output with the specific failure, and treat it as an open question to investigate.\n\nSo the concrete answer:\n\nWhen something failed, attach:\n1. The command you ran (e.g., `ctest --test-dir build/debug --output-on-failure`).\n2. The full red output — specifically the part that shows WHICH test failed and WHY:\n   - For ctest: the \"The following tests FAILED:\" list + the diagnostic output from `--output-on-failure` (the diff, the byte offset, the `FAIL err: \"...\"` line, exit codes).\n   - The summary line (\"99% tests passed, 2 tests failed out of 504\") alone is NOT enough — that only says HOW MANY failed, not WHICH and WHY.\n\n3. Per line 243: a red test is an open question — you attach it AND say what you're going to do (investigate). Don't silently declare \"так и должно\" (that disables the check).\n\nAlso, from the negative-control principle: when a check is CREATED, you should attach a red run (proving it can fall) ALONGSIDE the green. So the red output isn't a failure of YOUR work — it's proof the check has teeth.\n\nLet me also connect to the specific ctest case: for the unit test, \"что упало\" = the `FAIL err: \"   \"` / `FAIL ok: \"...\"` line printed by the harness + the ctest \"Failed\" status. So the red output should include the specific case name/input that failed.\n\nLet me also check: is there a more specific instruction in 03-ocenka.md about what to attach? The user said AGENTS and 02-praktika updated. 03-ocenka is about evaluation. But the question is really about the \"канал приёмки\" for the \"Программа/код\" class, which is covered in 02-praktika.\n\nLet me formulate a precise, concise answer.\n\nActually, I want to make sure I understand the user's actual confusion. They said:\n\"если прогон чистый я прилагаю что 100% passed... кароче низ где написано 'все круто'. а если что-то упало, что мне надо прилагать?\"\n\nSo they understand: green run → attach the bottom summary \"100% passed, 0 failed\". They're asking: red run → attach what?\n\nAnswer: attach the red run's DIAGNOSTIC, not just the summary. Specifically:\n- The \"The following tests FAILED:\" list (which tests).\n- The per-test failure detail (what `--output-on-failure` prints: the check that failed, expected vs actual, the byte offset, the `FAIL` line).\n- The command that produced it.\n\nAnd the principle: red is not something to summarize away as \"упало N штук\" — the value is in WHICH and WHY.\n\nAlso, crucially, distinguish the two kinds of red:\n1. Intentional red (negative control) → attach green + red, this is the proof the check works.\n2. Unexpected red (real bug) → attach red output + your diagnosis/hypothesis, it's an open question per line 243.\n\nLet me write this concisely. The user wants a practical \"что прилагать\" answer.\n\nLet me give concrete examples with the ctest output format they know.\n\nI should be concise. Let me structure:\n\n1. Direct answer: attach the red diagnostic, not just \"упало\".\n2. What exactly: command + \"The following tests FAILED\" + the --output-on-failure detail (which check, expected vs actual).\n3. The two kinds of red (negative control vs real failure), and the rule: red test = open question, not \"так и должно\".\n\nLet me write.\n\nActually, let me reconsider — the user's phrasing \"низ где написано 'все круто'\" suggests they're treating the summary line as the deliverable. I should clarify that even for GREEN, the summary line is what you attach, but for RED, the summary line (\"2 tests failed\") is NOT enough — you need the detail.\n\nLet me be precise and practical.\n\nI'll also note: for the unit test specifically, the red output is the `FAIL ...` line(s) from the harness stderr (visible only with `--output-on-failure`), plus the ctest \"Failed\" per-test line. Without `--output-on-failure`, ctest only shows \"2/2 Test #504: parse_point_c ...***Failed\" and you won't see WHICH case. So `--output-on-failure` is mandatory for a useful red run.\n\nLet me write the answer.",
