 convey:\n\nThe \"empty\" detection is tied to a SPECIFIC exit code semantics (66 no_input vs 65 data), which DIFFERS between tasks. If parse_int32 returns a generic `empty` code, then main still needs to map that code to the right exit code (66 vs 65) AND produce the right diagnostic message. So moving \"empty\" into parse_int32 does NOT remove the main-side logic — it just changes where the empty is DETECTED.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. What would parse_int32 gain by detecting empty?\n\nCurrently:\n- main: read → trim → if empty → print message, return exit code → else parse_int32.\n\nIf parse_int32 detects empty and returns `number_error::empty`:\n- main: read → trim → parse_int32 → if error == empty → print message, return exit code → else ...\n\nThe main still needs to handle the `empty` error code (print message + exit code). So the main logic doesn't get much simpler; it just moves the \"is it empty\" test from a direct string check to a \"what error code did parse_int32 return\" check.\n\nBUT there's a real benefit: if parse_int32 trims AND detects empty internally, then main doesn't need to trim first. So the sequence becomes:\n- main: read → parse_int32(line) → parse_int32 trims internally, detects empty → returns empty/not_number/out_of_range/ok → main maps error → message + exit code.\n\nThis consolidates: trim + empty-check + number-parse all in one place (parse_int32). Main just calls parse_int32 and maps the result.\n\nThis is actually a cleaner design IF parse_int32 is made fully self-contained (trim + empty + parse). That's option (b) from my previous answer, extended to also detect empty.\n\nBut wait — there's the diagnostic message problem. The message for \"empty number\" needs to show context (what line number, what was the input). The message for \"not a number\" shows the received value. If parse_int32 returns just a code, main produces the message. That's fine — main still owns the message.\n\nSo the design question boils down to:\n1. Should parse_int32 trim internally? (self-containment)\n2. Should parse_int32 distinguish \"empty\" (returns `empty` code) from \"not a number\" (returns `not_number`)?\n\nCurrently `empty` code exists but parse_int32 doesn't use it. parse_radius (t4) uses it.\n\nLet me think about the RECOMMENDATION.\n\nMy honest recommendation: There's a subtle but important distinction between \"empty\" and \"not a number\", and whether it's worth a separate code depends on whether the CONSUMER needs to distinguish them.\n\nIn t1/t2:\n- empty N/vertexes → a specific message (\"число не введено\" / \"EOF\" distinction) and exit code.\n- non-number → \"не число\" message, exit code.\n\nActually, wait — let me reconsider the EOF vs empty distinction. There's:\n- EOF (no line at all) → NO_INPUT (66).\n- empty line (just Enter) → ... depends on task.\n\nThis EOF-vs-empty distinction is already handled at the getline/get_string level (before trim). So parse_int32 only sees the \"empty after trim\" case (the actual empty string or whitespace), not EOF.\n\nSo parse_int32 receiving \"\" or \"   \" (whitespace) → currently returns not_number (from_chars invalid_argument). The user wants it to return `empty` instead, so main can distinguish \"empty number\" from \"non-number\".\n\nIs that distinction valuable? In t2, the messages are different:\n- \"Кол-во вершин должно быть целым числом. Получено: ...\" (non-number)\n- empty vertexes → different message.\n\nLet me check the actual messages. From t2_passport_cpp/main.cpp (read earlier):\n```cpp\ntrim_str(vertexes);\nif (vertexes.empty()) {\n    std::cerr << \"Ошибка. Пустое поле количества вершин\\n\";  // (mojibake)\n    return exit_code::data;\n}\nauto parsed = parse_int32(vertexes);\nif (!parsed) {\n    if (parsed.error() == number_error::out_of_range) { ... }\n    std::cerr << \"Кол-во вершин должно быть целым числом. Получено: \" << vertexes << \"\\n\";\n    return exit_code::data;\n}\n```\n\nSo currently: empty → \"пустое поле\" message,