d argue these are separate concerns.\n\nActually, for this project's scale and the README policy (\"краевые пробелы отбрасываются\"), consolidating into parse_int32 is reasonable and reduces caller burden.\n\nLet me also consider: the C side. Currently C parse_int32 uses strtol which ALREADY skips leading whitespace. So C parse_int32 is already \"partially trimming\" (leading). To make it symmetric with C++, both should trim fully (leading + trailing) + detect empty.\n\nLet me formulate my recommendation clearly.\n\nMy recommendation: **Yes, this is a reasonable consolidation, and it's option (b) from before extended with empty detection.** Make parse_int32 self-contained: trim edge whitespace, and distinguish `empty` (no digits at all, i.e., empty/whitespace-only) from `not_number` (has non-numeric junk). This:\n1. Removes the caller's trim + empty-check duplication (t1/t2 main become simpler).\n2. Uses the already-existing `empty` code (currently only parse_radius uses it).\n3. Makes C and C++ symmetric (both trim + detect empty).\n\nBut I should flag the tradeoffs/axes BEFORE recommending, per the anti-flattery block:\n\n**Axes:**\n1. **Boundary of validation**: currently trim+empty in main, parse in parse_int32. Moving = parse_int32 owns trim+empty+parse.\n2. **Error model granularity**: adding `empty` as a distinct code (it already exists in the enum) means parse_int32's return model becomes {ok, empty, not_number, out_of_range}. Consumers (t1/t2 main) map these to messages + exit codes.\n3. **Symmetry C/C++**: C strtol already skips leading whitespace; to match C++ (from_chars doesn't skip), BOTH need explicit trim for consistency. Currently they're asymmetric at the parse_int32 level but symmetric at the caller level (both trim in main).\n\n**Question to resolve:** does \"empty\" need to be distinct from \"not_number\"? \n- In t2: empty → \"пустое поле количества вершин\", non-number → \"должно быть целым числом\". Different messages → yes, worth distinguishing.\n- In t1: empty → NO_INPUT (66), non-number → DATA (65). Different EXIT CODES → definitely worth distinguishing.\n\nWait, let me verify t1's empty vs non-number exit codes. From cases.cmake:\n- t1_c_empty → NO_INPUT (66).\n- t1_c_abc → DATA (65).\n- t1_c_float → DATA (65).\n\nSo t1: empty → 66, non-number → 65. Different exit codes! So parse_int32 distinguishing `empty` from `not_number` is genuinely useful for t1 (map empty → 66, not_number → 65).\n\nActually wait — is that right? Let me reconsider t1's semantics. t1 reads a single N. Empty input → \"no input\" (66). Non-number → \"data\" (65). So yes, the empty-vs-nonnumber distinction maps to different exit codes in t1.\n\nBut hold on — there's also the EOF case. In t1, EOF (no line) and empty line (whitespace/empty after trim) — are they both \"empty\" (66)? From the case `t1_c_empty` uses `t1_nul` input (empty). And there might be a separate EOF handling. Let me not over-analyze; the point is that \"empty number\" and \"non-number\" are distinct classes with different exit codes/messages.\n\nSo my recommendation: moving empty detection into parse_int32 (returning a distinct `empty` code) is a GOOD idea, and here's why + what it entails.\n\nLet me also consider the practical scope: this is a refactor touching:\n- common/string_utils.cpp parse_int32 (add trim + empty detection).\n- common/string_utils.c parse_int32 (add trim + empty detection, for symmetry — though C strtol already skips leading, need trailing trim + empty).\n- t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.cpp and t1_dist_matrix_c/main.c (remove trim_str + empty check, handle empty code).\n- t2_passport_cpp/main.cpp and t2_passport_c/main.c (same).\n- Update unit tests (parse_int32) to cover empty and space cases.\n- Update expected messages/exit codes if they change (they shouldn't if we keep the same messages).\n\nThis is a non-trivial refactor. But it's a coherent one.\n\nLet me also think: is there a RISK? The main risk is that \"empty\" vs \"not_number\" boundary needs careful definition:\n- \"\" → empty.\n- \"   \" → empty (all whitespace).\n- \"x\" → not_number (has junk, no digits).\n- \"5x\" → not_number.\n- \"  5x  \" → after trim \"5x\" → not_number.\n- \"5\" → ok.\n\nSo \"empty\" = no digits at all (after trim, empty). \"not_number\" = has some content but not a valid number.\n\nThis is clean.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtlety with the EOF distinction. In t1/t2, EOF is detected at the getline/get_string level (returned as a special case), NOT by parse_int32. So parse_int32 never sees EOF — it only sees the actual string (possibly empty after a successful read). The EOF vs empty distinction stays in main (getline returns false → EOF → no_input). So parse_int32's \"empty\" is specifically \"the string (after trim) has no digits\", which maps to the \"empty line\" case, distinct from EOF.\n\nThis is important to note: parse_int32's `empty` ≠ EOF. EOF is a read-level concern, handled by getline/get_string. So moving empty-detection into parse_int32 does NOT conflate empty with EOF.\n\nOK, I think I have a clear picture. Let me formulate the recommendation.\n\nActually, let me also verify the current t1/t2 flow by reading the files, to make sure I'm not misremembering. Let me read t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.cpp and t2_passport_cpp/main.cpp (C++ side is enough for the recommendation, plus I know C mirrors it).\n\nActually, I already have enough from the grep and earlier reads. Let me read t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.cpp to confirm the empty handling, and t2 I already read fully earlier (t2_passport_cpp/main.cpp has trim_str + empty check + parse_int32).\n\nLet me read t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.cpp to be precise.\n\nActually, let me be efficient. I have:\n- t2_passport_cpp/main.cpp (read earlier): trim_str(vertexes), if empty → \"пустое поле\", parse_int32 → error handling.\n- t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.cpp: from grep, line 21 trim_str(lineN), line 28 parse_int32(lineN). Need to see the empty check.\n\nLet me read t1_dist_matrix_cpp