ading whitespace, `first` can point at the first non-space, and strtol parses from there.\n\nWait, let me think. If I do:\n```c\nconst char* first = s;\nwhile (isspace(*first)) ++first;  // skip leading\nconst char* last = first + strlen(first);\nwhile (last > first && isspace(last[-1])) --last;  // skip trailing\nif (first == last) return NUMBER_EMPTY;\n// now [first, last) is the trimmed number\nerrno = 0;\nchar* end = NULL;\nlong v = strtol(first, &end, 10);\nif (errno == ERANGE || v < INT32_MIN || v > INT32_MAX) return OUT_OF_RANGE;\nif (end != last) return NOT_NUMBER;  // end should equal last (all chars consumed)\n*out = v; return OK;\n```\n\nThis is non-mutating and correct. `strtol(first, &end, 10)` skips leading whitespace (but `first` already points at non-space), parses the number, `end` points at the first char after the number (which should be `last` if the whole [first, last) is a valid number).\n\nWait, but there's the `+` handling. The current C parse_int32 has a special `+` check:\n```c\nif (*first == '+') {\n    ++first;\n    if (!isdigit(*first)) return NOT_NUMBER;\n}\n```\nThis is to reject \"+-5\" (strtol would accept \"+\" then see \"-\" and parse nothing, returning 0 with end==first... actually strtol(\"+-5\") returns 0 and end==nptr). Hmm, actually the `+` check is a bit of a special case from the strtol/from_chars symmetry work.\n\nAnyway, this is getting into implementation details. My job here is to REPORT the bugs, not write the fix (plan mode).\n\nLet me organize the findings clearly and concisely.\n\nActually, let me also note one more thing: the C++ parse_int32, after fixing `is_empty(s)` → `s.empty()`, is actually CORRECT. Let me verify the full logic:\n\n```cpp\nstd::expected<std::int32_t, number_error> parse_int32(std::string_view s) {\n    while (!s.empty() && isspace(s.front())) s.remove_prefix(1);   // leading\n    while (!s.empty() && isspace(s.back())) s.remove_suffix(1);    // trailing\n    if (s.empty()) return unexpected(emp