d still reject \"5 \" because strtol stops at the space, and the space is not '\\0'.\n\nTo correctly accept trailing whitespace, the check should be `end_recogn != end` (end_recogn should equal the trimmed-end position), NOT `*end_recogn != '\\0'`.\n\nHmm, so the user's C parse_int32 approach is fundamentally incomplete: it computes `start`/`end` but then uses strtol with the '\\0' check, which doesn't account for trailing whitespace.\n\nWait, but actually — let me reconsider. The C++ side trims the VIEW (remove_prefix/remove_suffix), so from_chars operates on the trimmed range [first, last), and `ptr != last` correctly checks that the ENTIRE trimmed range was consumed.\n\nThe C side can't \"trim the view\" the same way (C strings are null-terminated). So to match, the C side needs to either:\n(a) Null-terminate at `end` (mutation — but that's the const issue), OR\n(b) Check `end_recogn != end` (strtol consumed up to the trimmed end).\n\nThe user's current code does NEITHER — it computes `start`/`end` but then checks `*end_recogn != '\\0'` (option neither). So trailing whitespace is rejected.\n\nSo the C parse_int32 is broken in MULTIPLE ways:\n1. `end` is `char` not `char*` (compile error).\n2. Trailing trim loop starts at '\\0' (never trims).\n3. The final check `*end_recogn != '\\0'` doesn't account for trailing whitespace (should be `end_recogn != end`).\n\nActually, let me reconsider #3. If we fix #1 and #2 (end becomes const char* and points at the last non-space char), then:\n- For \"5 \" (trailing space): `end` should point at '5' (the last non-space char), i.e., `end = s+1` (position of '5').\n- strtol(\"5 \", &end_recogn, 10) → parses \"5\", end_recogn = s+1 (the space).\n- Check should be `end_recogn != end` → end_recogn (s+1, space) vs end (s+1, '5')? Wait, this is getting confusing because of off-by-one.\n\nLet me define clearly. Let `end` = pointer to the position AFTER the last non-space char (i.e., where the trimmed number ends). Then:\n- \"5\" → end