ase=8 or 0. But parse_int32 uses base=10 (strtol(first, &end, 10)). So \"09\" → 9 (base 10, leading zero ignored). And from_chars<int32_t> → 9. So \"09\" → 9. ✓ (no octal issue since base is explicitly 10).\n\nActually, let me not overthink. Let me build a comprehensive but focused set of cases, symmetric between C and C++, that covers:\n1. Norm (ok): basic integers, +, -, 0, leading zeros, edge spaces, int32 min/max.\n2. Empty: \"\", \"   \", whitespace-only.\n3. not_number: non-digit, trailing junk, inner space, hex, ++, +-, \"+\", \"+ abc\".\n4. out_of_range: INT32_MAX+1, INT32_MIN-1, big.\n\nBut I need to be careful about symmetry between C (strtol) and C++ (from_chars). Let me think about where they might diverge:\n\n- Leading spaces: C strtol skips, but parse_int32 (C) now explicitly trims leading/trailing, so both should handle \" 5 \" → 5. ✓ (after the trim fix).\n- \"+\" handling: both have explicit \"+\" checks. For \"+\", \"++\", \"+-\", \"+abc\" → not_number.\n- \"-\" alone: \"-\" → strtol(\"-\") → 0, end==start → not_number. from_chars(\"-\") → invalid_argument → not_number. Both not_number. ✓ (No explicit \"-\" handling needed? Actually \"-5\" → both parse -5 fine. \"-\" alone → not_number.)\n\nWait, let me check the C++ parse_int32 for \"-\" handling. The C++ code has:\n```cpp\nif (first != last && *first == '+') {\n    ++first;\n    if (first == last || !std::isdigit(*first))\n        return not_number;\n}\n```\nIt only special-cases '+', not '-'. For \"-5\": from_chars(\"-5\") → -5. For \"-\" alone: from_chars(\"-\") → invalid_argument → not_number. For \"--5\": from_chars(\"--5\") → invalid_argument → not_number. So C++ handles '-' via from_chars naturally.\n\nFor C: strtol(\"-5\") → -5. strtol(\"-\") → 0, end==start → not_number. strtol(\"--5\") → 0, end==start → not_number. So C handles '-' via strtol naturally.\n\nSo both handle '-' consistently.\n\nNow the \"+\" special case: why does the code special-case '+'? Because from_chars and strtol differ on \"+-5\":\n- from_chars(\"+-5\") → invalid_argument (from_chars rejects leading +). Wait, actually from_chars doesn't accept leading + at all. So \"+-5\" → from_chars sees '+', which is not a valid start → invalid_argument → not_number.\n- strtol(\"+-5\") → skips '+', sees '-', parses nothing → 0, end==start → not_number.\n\nWait, but actually the concern (from the journal) was about \"+-5\" where strtol might... hmm. Let me recall. The journal 09-18/09-19 discussed strtol vs from_chars differences:\n- from_chars doesn't accept leading '+'.\n- strtol DOES accept leading '+'.\n\nSo for \"+5\": from_chars(\"+5\") → invalid_argument (rejects +). strtol(\"+5\") → 5.\n\nSo WITHOUT the explicit '+' handling, C++ would reject \"+5\" (from_chars rejects +) while C would accept it (strtol accepts +). That's why the explicit '+' check was added: to make C++ accept \"+5\" (by skipping the + before from_chars).\n\nSo the '+' handling:\n- \"+5\" → both skip + → 5. ✓\n- \"+-5\" → C++: skip +, then isdigit('-')? No → not_number. C: skip +, isdigit('-')? No → not_number. ✓\n- \"++5\" → C++: skip +, isdigit('+')? No → not_number. C: same. ✓\n- \"+\" alone → C++: skip +, first==last → not_number. C: skip +, isdigit('\\0')? No → not_number. ✓\n- \"+abc\" → not_number. ✓\n\nSo the '+' handling makes both consistent for \"+5\", \"+-5\", \"++5\", \"+\", \"+abc\".\n\nNow, are there OTHER divergences I need to worry about for the test cases?\n\n- Hex \"0x10\": C strtol(\"0x10\", base 10) → parses \"0\", end at \"x\" → not_number (since *end != '\\0'... wait, but now the check is `end_recogn != end`, where end is the trimmed end). Let me trace \"0x10\" with the NEW C parse_int32:\n  - start = \"0x10\" (no leading space). end = start + strlen(\"0x10\") = start+4. trailing: isspace(end[-1]='0') false → no back up. end = start+4.\n  - '+'? no.\n  - strtol(\"0x10\", base 10) → parses \"0\", end_recogn = start+1 ('x'). \n  - `end_recogn != end` → start+1 != start+4 → not_number. ✓\n- C++ \"0x10\": from_chars(\"0x10\") → parses \"0\", ptr at \"x\" != last → not_number. ✓\n\nSo \"0x10\" → not_number both. ✓\n\n- \"5.5\": C strtol(\"5.5\", 10) → 5, end at \".\" → end_recogn != end → not_number. C++ from_chars → 5, ptr at \".\" → not_number. ✓\n\n- Inner space \"5 5\": \n  - C: start=\"5 5\", end=start+3. strtol(\"5 5\") → 5, end_recogn at \" \" (start+1). `end_recogn != end` → not_number. ✓\n  - C++: from_chars(\"5 5\") → 5, ptr at \" \" != last → not_number. ✓\n\n- Trailing space \"5 \": \n  - C (new): end backs up past the trailing space. start=\"5 \", end = start + strlen(\"5 \") = start+2, back up: isspace(end[-1]=' ') → --end → start+1. isspace(end[-1]='5') false. end=start+1. strtol(\"5 \") → 5, end_recogn=start+1. end_recogn==end → OK → 5. ✓\n  - C++: remove_suffix → \"5\", from_chars → 5. ✓\n\n- Leading+trailing \" 5 \": both → 5. ✓\n\nSo the cases are symmetric.\n\nNow, let me also think about int32 min/max:\n- \"2147483647\" (INT32_MAX): C strtol on Windows (long==int32) → 2147483647, no ERANGE. v == INT32_MAX, not > INT32_MAX → not out_of_range. end_recogn == end → OK → 2147483647. On Linux (long 64-bit): strtol → 2147483647, v < INT32_MAX+1 → OK. So \"2147483647\" → 2147483647. C++ from_chars → 2147483647. ✓\n- \"-2147483648\" (INT32_MIN): C on Windows: strtol(\"-2147483648\") → -2147483648 (fits int32? -2147483648 is INT32_MIN, which is -2147483648. strtol returns LONG_MIN = -2147483648 on Windows since long==32-bit. No ERANGE because -2147483648 IS representable in 32-bit long). v == INT32_MIN, not < INT32_MIN → OK. On Linux (64-bit): strtol → -2147483648, v >= INT32_MIN → OK. So \"-2147483648\" → -2147483648. C++ from_chars → -2147483648. ✓\n- \"2147483648\" (INT32_MAX+1): C on Windows: strtol → LONG_MAX (2147483647) + ERANGE → out_of_range. On Linux: strtol → 2147483648, v > INT32_MAX → out_of_range. C++ from_chars → out_of_range. ✓\n- \"-2147483649\" (INT32_MIN-1): similar → out_of_range. ✓\n\nSo these are symmetric.\n\nNow, a subtle one: the C++ from_chars<int32_t> for \"2147483648\" — does it return result_out_of_range? Yes, from_chars for integer overflow returns result_out_of_range. ✓\n\nOK, so I have a good understanding. Let me now design the case lists.\n\n**parse_int32** (both C and C++):\n\nok (check_ok):\n- \"5\" → 5\n- \"+7\" → 7\n- \"-3\" → -3\n- \"0\" → 0\n- \"007\" → 7 (leading zeros)\n- \"2147483647\" → 2147483647 (INT32_MAX)\n- \"-2147483648\" → -2147483648 (INT32_MIN)\n- \" 42 \" → 42 (edge spaces)\n- \" +7 \" → 7 (spaces + plus)\n\nempty (check_err → empty):\n- \"\" → empty\n- \"   \" → empty\n- \"\\t\\n\" → empty (whitespace-only, tab+newline)\n\nnot_number (check_err → not_number):\n- \"abc\" → not_number\n- \"5x\" → not_number\n- \"5.5\" → not_number\n- \"0x10\" → not_number\n- \"5 5\" → not_number (inner space)\n- \"+-5\" → not_number\n- \"++5\" → not_number\n- \"+\" → not_number\n- \"+abc\" → not_number\n\nout_of_range (check_err → out_of_range):\n- \"2147483648\" → out_of_range\n- \"-2147483649\" → out_of_range\n- \"3000000000\" → out_of_range\n\nHmm, that's a lot. But the user said \"добавим все тест-кейсы ... как делали это для parse_point\". For parse_point, I added a comprehensive set (11 ok + ~24 err cases). So \"все тест-кейсы\" means a comprehensive set.\n\nBut wait — the user earlier said \"пока не делай там много кейсов, только когда я попрошу об этом явно\". Now they're explicitly asking to add all test cases. So now I SHOULD add a comprehensive set.\n\nLet me be reasonable and add a solid, comprehensive set, symmetric C/C++, mirroring the acceptance corpus. Let me not go overboard but cover all the error classes and key corner cases.\n\nLet me define the final case list for each function.\n\nActually, I need to check what's CURRENTLY in the files (I wrote minimal cases earlier, and the user added some). Let me read the current state of all 7 files first.\n\nLet me read:\n1. tests/unit/parse_int32.cpp\n2. tests/unit/parse_int32.c\n3. tests/unit/is_empty.cpp\n4. tests/unit/is_empty.c\n5. tests/unit/is_hex_prefix.c\n6. tests/unit/trim_string.cpp\n7. tests/unit/trim_string.c\n\nThen add comprehensive cases.\n\nLet me read them all.\n\nActually, I already know roughly what's in them (I wrote minimal cases earlier, and the user added empty/space cases to parse_int32). But let me read the current state to be precise, especially parse_int32 which the user modified.\n\nLet me read all 7 files.\n\nLet me think about the trim_string/is_empty/is_hex_prefix cases too.\n\n**is_empty** (returns 1 if empty/all-whitespace, 0 otherwise):\n- \"\" → 1\n- \"   \" → 1\n- \"\\t\\n\" → 1 (whitespace)\n- \"abc\" → 0\n- \"  a\" → 0 (leading space + content)\n- \"a  \" → 0 (content + trailing space)\n- \" a \" → 0\n- \"a\" → 0\n- \"0\" → 0 (digit is non-space)\n\nNote: is_empty uses isspace, which handles \\t \\n \\r \\v \\f and space. So \"\\t\\n\" → 1, \"\\v\\f\" → 1.\n\nActually, is_empty C++ uses `find_first_not_space` which uses `std::isspace`. C uses `isspace`. Both handle the standard whitespace set.\n\nSo cases:\n- \"\" → 1\n- \"   \" → 1\n- \"\\t\\n\" → 1\n- \"abc\" → 0\n- \"  a\" → 0\n- \"0\" → 0\n\n**is_hex_prefix** (C only, returns 1 if \"0x\"/\"0X\" prefix after leading spaces and optional +/-):\n- \"0x10\" → 1\n- \"0X10\" → 1\n- \"  0x10\" → 1 (leading spaces)\n- \"+0x10\" → 1 (plus)\n- \"-0X10\" → 1 (minus)\n- \"0x\" → 1 (just \"0x\")\n- \"10\" → 0\n- \"0\" → 0\n- \"abc\" → 0\n- \"\" → 0\n- \"   \" → 0 (whitespace only, no 0x)\n\nWait, let me check \"   \" (whitespace only) for is_hex_prefix:\n```c\nint is_hex_prefix(const char* ptr) {\n    while (isspace((unsigned char)*ptr)) ++ptr;\n    if (*ptr == '\\0') return 0;\n    int pad = 0;\n    if (*ptr == '+' || *ptr == '-') ++pad;\n    if (*(ptr + pad) == '0' && (*(ptr + pad + 1) == 'x' || *(ptr + pad + 1) == 'X'))\n        return 1;\n    return 0;\n}\n```\nFor \"   \": skip spaces → '\\0' → return 0. ✓\nFor \"0x10\": '0' then 'x' → 1. ✓\nFor \"+0x10\": '+' → pad=1, *(ptr+1)='0', *(ptr+2)='x' → 1. ✓\nFor \"-0X10\": '-' → pad=1, *(ptr+1)='0', *(ptr+2)='X' → 1. ✓\nFor \"0x\": '0' then 'x' → 1. ✓ (just prefix detection)\nFor \"10\": '1' not 0x → 0. ✓\nFor \"0\": '0', *(ptr+1)='\\0' not 'x'/'X' → 0. ✓\nFor \"0xg\": '0' then 'x' → 1 (it's a hex prefix even if the hex digits are invalid). ✓\n\nSo cases:\n- \"0x10\" → 1\n- \"0X10\" → 1\n- \"  0x10\" → 1\n- \"+0x10\" → 1\n- \"-0X10\" → 1\n- \"0x\" → 1\n- \"10\" → 0\n- \"0\" → 0\n- \"\" → 0\n- \"   \" → 0\n- \"abc\" → 0\n\n**trim_string / trim_str** (trims edge whitespace):\n- \"  abc  \" → \"abc\"\n- \"abc\" → \"abc\" (no change)\n- \"   \" → \"\" (all spaces → empty)\n- \"\" → \"\" (empty)\n- \"\\tabc\\n\" → \"abc\" (tab/newline edges)\n- \"  a b  \" → \"a b\" (inner space preserved)\n- \" a \" → \"a\"\n\nLet me think about C trim_string and C++ trim_str semantics.\n\nC trim_string (mutates, returns pointer):\n```c\nchar* trim_string(char* s, int* len) {\n    while (*len > 0 && isspace((unsigned char)*s)) { ++s; --(*len); }\n    while (*len > 0 && isspace((unsigned char)s[*len - 1])) { --(*len); s[*len] = '\\0'; }\n    return s;\n}\n```\nIt advances `s` past leading spaces and sets `*len` to trimmed length, null-terminating at the trimmed end.\n\nC++ trim_str:\n```cpp\nvoid trim_str(std::string& s) {\n    auto left = find_first_not_space(s);\n    if (left == npos) { s.clear(); return; }\n    s.erase(0, left);\n    auto right = find_last_not_space(s);\n    s.erase(right + 1);\n}\n```\n\nBoth use isspace (so \\t \\n \\r \\v \\f are trimmed).\n\nTest cases (for the helper `check(input, expected)`):\n- \"  abc  \" → \"abc\"\n- \"abc\" → \"abc\"\n- \"   \" → \"\"\n- \"\" → \"\"\n- \" a \" → \"a\"\n- \"  a b  \" → \"a b\" (inner space preserved)\n- \"\\tabc\\n\" → \"abc\"\n\nNote: for C trim_string, I need to build a mutable buffer. The current test does:\n```c\nstatic void check(const char* in, const char* want) {\n    char buf[64];\n    int len = (int)strlen(in);\n    memcpy(buf, in, (size_t)len + 1);\n    const char* res = trim_string(buf, &len);\n    if (strcmp(res, want) != 0) { ... }\n}\n```\n\nThis works for all the cases (buf[64] is big enough).\n\nFor C++ trim_str:\n```cpp\nstatic void check(std::string in, const std::string& want) {\n    trim_str(in);\n    if (in != want) { ... }\n}\n```\n\nThis works.\n\nNow let me also think about whether to include \"\\v\" and \"\\f\" (vertical tab, form feed) in is_empty/trim tests, since isspace handles them. The journal mentioned \\v/\\f as a regression. For is_empty and trim_string, isspace handles \\v/\\f. But in the test string literals, \"\\v\" and \"\\f\" are escape sequences. I could add \"\\v\\f\" → empty for is_empty, and \"\\vabc\\f\" → \"abc\" for trim. But these are edge cases. Let me include a couple to be thorough, since the user said \"все тест-кейсы\" (comprehensive).\n\nActually, let me keep it reasonable. I'll include \"\\t\\n\" (tab+newline) which is common, and maybe \"\\v\\f\" too for is_empty. Let me include the standard whitespace.\n\nLet me now write the case lists and edit the files.\n\nLet me first read the current state of all 7 files to see what's there.\n\nActually, I already know the current state from my earlier writes + the user's additions. But the user added cases to parse_int32. Let me read all 7 to be precise.\n\nLet me read them.",
